{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Xbt5nHKhaq7mpPrqaDrWZo","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have the full paper (9 pages) and review context. Dispatching two reviewers in parallel: R1 (Methods + Experiments) and R2 (Novelty + Related Work)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":32028,"cache_read_input_tokens":22958,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":32028},"output_tokens":19,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3dbddd0c-fe11-423e-bc66-5161ab0e513e","uuid":"1d57c61f-b0a2-4fe3-b078-508e4a36539a"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Xbt5nHKhaq7mpPrqaDrWZo","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01SfVBGkmTnqCYQUegZczMa7","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\nTitle: \"Empirical Evidence Toward Boundary Conditions for Error Injection in LLM Agent Training: A Negative Transfer Study\"\n\nThe paper investigates whether taxonomy-guided error injection (shown effective in tool-use via PALADIN on ToolBench) transfers to embodied planning (ALFWorld). They design TaxEI, a 4-operator error injection taxonomy, and train Qwen3-8B under three SFT conditions: Vanilla (clean data, 4358 samples), TaxEI (clean + taxonomy-labeled injections, 9029 samples), Random (clean + unlabeled injections, 9029 samples). Trained with LoRA (r=16, α=32) across 5 seeds on Qwen3-8B, 3 seeds on LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct.\n\n## Key Results\n\n1. **Main (5-seed Qwen3-8B, 47 test tasks)**: Both injection methods degrade by 9.0pp vs Vanilla (67.3% → 58.3%). Cohen's d=1.58, Welch t p=0.03 uncorrected but Bonferroni NS (α=0.017). Statistical power = 45%.\n2. **Taxonomy = zero benefit**: TaxEI and Random both = 58.3%.\n3. **Resilience not recovery**: Recovery rate ≈ Clean rate for all conditions (Δ < 1pp). Detection rate = 0% across all.\n4. **Cross-model (LLaMA, 3-seed)**: Directional consistency. TaxEI SD=8.6pp vs Random SD=2.1pp. 1/3 catastrophic seed (s123: 59.6%). Detection 27% but doesn't help recovery. Welch t p=0.52 (far from sig).\n5. **Scale ablation (500→2500 traj)**: Gap narrows 9.0→6.4pp (29% reduction). Catastrophic seeds eliminated. Cohen's d drops 1.58→1.05. Welch t p=0.065.\n6. **Few-shot recovery prompting**: All conditions benefit equally (+4-6pp), ANOVA p=0.78. Detection 0%→~15%.\n7. **Per-operator (exploratory)**: Directional pattern (local operators neutral, global operators harmful) but all Fisher p>0.38. Seed=42 C4 +9.1pp collapses to +1.8pp at 5-seed.\n\n## Tables\n- Table 1: 5-seed recovery eval (V/T/R clean vs recovery ± SD, detection)\n- Table 2: Cross-model comparison (Qwen 5s vs LLaMA 3s)\n- Table 3: LLaMA per-seed breakdown\n- Table 4: Scale ablation (500 vs 2500 traj)\n- Table 5: Diminishing returns in gap reduction\n- Table 6: Few-shot recovery prompting\n- Table 7: Per-operator analysis (exploratory)\n- Figure 1: Power curve\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\n\nThe experimental design document lists 6 imagined experiments:\n1. Recovery Eval Multi-Seed (5-seed × 3 conditions) → Covered in Table 1\n2. LLaMA Cross-Model Replication (3-seed) → Covered in Tables 2-3\n3. Scale Ablation (25K–50K trajectories) → Covered in Tables 4-5\n4. Recovery Prompting (Few-Shot) → Covered in Table 6\n5. Per-Operator Deep Dive (Multi-Seed) → Covered in Table 7\n6. Scale-Up Data Collection → Covered in Appendix B\n\nAll 6 imagined experiments are present in the paper. Coverage is complete.\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the paper on:\n1. **Experimental methodology**: Are conditions properly controlled? Are comparisons fair? Is the evaluation protocol sound?\n2. **Statistical rigor**: Are tests appropriate? Is the honesty about power/significance appropriate or does it undermine claims?\n3. **Data volume confound**: Vanilla=4358 samples, TaxEI/Random=9029. How much does this matter?\n4. **Cross-model validity**: n=3 seeds on LLaMA — does this add value or is it too weak?\n5. **Scale ablation interpretation**: Does 29% gap reduction at 5× scale undermine the task-structure hypothesis?\n6. **Missing experiments or controls**: What's absent that would strengthen claims?\n7. **Reproducibility**: Is there enough detail to replicate?\n\nProduce a structured review with:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (numbered list)\n- Weaknesses (numbered list, with severity: major/minor)\n- Questions for Authors (numbered)\n- Missing Experiments / Suggestions\n- Score (1-6) with justification\n- Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. This is a negative-result paper — evaluate whether the negative result is convincingly established and whether the boundary-condition framework adds value."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":32028,"cache_read_input_tokens":22958,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":32028},"output_tokens":19,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3dbddd0c-fe11-423e-bc66-5161ab0e513e","uuid":"9bffe987-70d6-4c6f-8ee2-ee78082d2986"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"ad737ab2d99ea083c","tool_use_id":"toolu_01SfVBGkmTnqCYQUegZczMa7","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\nTitle: \"Empirical Evidence Toward Boundary Conditions for Error Injection in LLM Agent Training: A Negative Transfer Study\"\n\nThe paper investigates whether taxonomy-guided error injection (shown effective in tool-use via PALADIN on ToolBench) transfers to embodied planning (ALFWorld). They design TaxEI, a 4-operator error injection taxonomy, and train Qwen3-8B under three SFT conditions: Vanilla (clean data, 4358 samples), TaxEI (clean + taxonomy-labeled injections, 9029 samples), Random (clean + unlabeled injections, 9029 samples). Trained with LoRA (r=16, α=32) across 5 seeds on Qwen3-8B, 3 seeds on LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct.\n\n## Key Results\n\n1. **Main (5-seed Qwen3-8B, 47 test tasks)**: Both injection methods degrade by 9.0pp vs Vanilla (67.3% → 58.3%). Cohen's d=1.58, Welch t p=0.03 uncorrected but Bonferroni NS (α=0.017). Statistical power = 45%.\n2. **Taxonomy = zero benefit**: TaxEI and Random both = 58.3%.\n3. **Resilience not recovery**: Recovery rate ≈ Clean rate for all conditions (Δ < 1pp). Detection rate = 0% across all.\n4. **Cross-model (LLaMA, 3-seed)**: Directional consistency. TaxEI SD=8.6pp vs Random SD=2.1pp. 1/3 catastrophic seed (s123: 59.6%). Detection 27% but doesn't help recovery. Welch t p=0.52 (far from sig).\n5. **Scale ablation (500→2500 traj)**: Gap narrows 9.0→6.4pp (29% reduction). Catastrophic seeds eliminated. Cohen's d drops 1.58→1.05. Welch t p=0.065.\n6. **Few-shot recovery prompting**: All conditions benefit equally (+4-6pp), ANOVA p=0.78. Detection 0%→~15%.\n7. **Per-operator (exploratory)**: Directional pattern (local operators neutral, global operators harmful) but all Fisher p>0.38. Seed=42 C4 +9.1pp collapses to +1.8pp at 5-seed.\n\n## Tables\n- Table 1: 5-seed recovery eval (V/T/R clean vs recovery ± SD, detection)\n- Table 2: Cross-model comparison (Qwen 5s vs LLaMA 3s)\n- Table 3: LLaMA per-seed breakdown\n- Table 4: Scale ablation (500 vs 2500 traj)\n- Table 5: Diminishing returns in gap reduction\n- Table 6: Few-shot recovery prompting\n- Table 7: Per-operator analysis (exploratory)\n- Figure 1: Power curve\n\n## Experiment Design Reference (from review_context.yaml)\n\nThe experimental design document lists 6 imagined experiments:\n1. Recovery Eval Multi-Seed (5-seed × 3 conditions) → Covered in Table 1\n2. LLaMA Cross-Model Replication (3-seed) → Covered in Tables 2-3\n3. Scale Ablation (25K–50K trajectories) → Covered in Tables 4-5\n4. Recovery Prompting (Few-Shot) → Covered in Table 6\n5. Per-Operator Deep Dive (Multi-Seed) → Covered in Table 7\n6. Scale-Up Data Collection → Covered in Appendix B\n\nAll 6 imagined experiments are present in the paper. Coverage is complete.\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nEvaluate the paper on:\n1. **Experimental methodology**: Are conditions properly controlled? Are comparisons fair? Is the evaluation protocol sound?\n2. **Statistical rigor**: Are tests appropriate? Is the honesty about power/significance appropriate or does it undermine claims?\n3. **Data volume confound**: Vanilla=4358 samples, TaxEI/Random=9029. How much does this matter?\n4. **Cross-model validity**: n=3 seeds on LLaMA — does this add value or is it too weak?\n5. **Scale ablation interpretation**: Does 29% gap reduction at 5× scale undermine the task-structure hypothesis?\n6. **Missing experiments or controls**: What's absent that would strengthen claims?\n7. **Reproducibility**: Is there enough detail to replicate?\n\nProduce a structured review with:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (numbered list)\n- Weaknesses (numbered list, with severity: major/minor)\n- Questions for Authors (nu