{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NS6DTwEHWvpzgxWehak9RU","type":"tool_result","content":"200\tPremature termination    & 4 & 16.7\\% & Partially \\\\\n201\tWrong object selection   & 4 & 16.7\\% & No \\\\\n202\tSame as vanilla failure  & 5 & 20.8\\% & No \\\\\n203\t\\bottomrule\n204\t\\end{tabular}\n205\t\\caption{Failure mode taxonomy from 24 analyzed TaxEI failure cases.}\n206\t\\label{tab:failure}\n207\t\\end{table}\n208\t\n209\tThe most diagnostic pattern is \\emph{operator replay}: TaxEI-trained models reproduce taxonomy-specific error behaviors during clean evaluation. For example, models skip cleaning steps (matching P2 sub-goal deletion patterns) or truncate context processing (matching C1). Of 24 cases, 15 (62.5\\%) are attributable to wrong learning (model internalized error patterns), 5 (20.8\\%) are indistinguishable from baseline noise, and 4 (16.7\\%) are ambiguous. This distribution supports the hypothesis that taxonomy labels teach specific wrong behaviors rather than merely adding noise, consistent with the structured gradient interference observed in \\S\\ref{sec:dynamics}.\n210\t\n211\t\\subsection{Second Environment: WebShop}\n212\t\\label{sec:webshop}\n213\t\n214\tTo test generalization, we replicate the core experiment on WebShop \\cite{yao2022webshop} with adapted error taxonomy and 2-seed evaluation (Table~\\ref{tab:webshop}).\n215\t\n216\t\\begin{table}[t]\n217\t\\centering\n218\t\\small\n219\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n220\t\\toprule\n221\t\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{Mean} & \\textbf{SD} & \\textbf{vs.\\ Vanilla} \\\\\n222\t\\midrule\n223\tVanilla & 58.5\\% & 2.8pp & --- \\\\\n224\tRandom  & 55.8\\% & 5.9pp & $-$2.7pp \\\\\n225\tTaxEI   & 51.2\\% & 7.4pp & $-$7.3pp \\\\\n226\t\\bottomrule\n227\t\\end{tabular}\n228\t\\caption{WebShop results (2-seed). The variance gradient (Vanilla $<$ Random $<$ TaxEI) replicates.}\n229\t\\label{tab:webshop}\n230\t\\end{table}\n231\t\n232\t\\begin{table}[t]\n233\t\\centering\n234\t\\small\n235\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n236\t\\toprule\n237\t\\textbf{Pattern} & \\textbf{ALFWorld} & \\textbf{WebShop} & \\textbf{Consistent?} \\\\\n238\t\\midrule\n239\tVariance gradient & 3.3$<$7.0$<$8.6 & 2.8$<$5.9$<$7.4 & \\checkmark \\\\\n240\tTaxEI vs.\\ Vanilla & $-$8.6pp & $-$7.3pp & \\checkmark \\\\\n241\tTax.\\ penalty & 6.4pp & 4.6pp & \\checkmark~(weaker) \\\\\n242\t10$\\to$25\\% drop & 12.8pp & 9.5pp & \\checkmark~(weaker) \\\\\n243\t\\bottomrule\n244\t\\end{tabular}\n245\t\\caption{Cross-environment pattern comparison. All directional findings replicate; magnitudes are weaker on WebShop, consistent with its denser reward signal.}\n246\t\\label{tab:crossenv}\n247\t\\end{table}\n248\t\n249\tThe variance gradient (Vanilla SD 2.8pp $<$ Random 5.9pp $<$ TaxEI 7.4pp) and taxonomy penalty (4.6pp) both replicate. The weaker magnitudes are consistent with WebShop's denser reward signal and more structured action space, which partially buffer against gradient interference. The dose-response threshold also replicates at a weaker level: 9.5pp drop from 10\\% to 25\\% injection. This cross-environment consistency supports the conclusion that the negative effects are task-structural rather than environment-specific.\n250\t\n251\t\\subsection{Scale Ablation}\n252\t\\label{sec:scale}\n253\t\n254\tA natural objection is that our training set (9K samples) is too small. We ablate scale at 25K and 50K trajectories (Table~\\ref{tab:scale}).\n255\t\n256\t\\begin{table}[t]\n257\t\\centering\n258\t\\small\n259\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n260\t\\toprule\n261\t\\textbf{Scale} & \\textbf{Vanilla} & \\textbf{TaxEI} & \\textbf{Random} & \\textbf{TaxEI$-$V} & \\textbf{Tax.\\ Pen.} \\\\\n262\t\\midrule\n263\t9K  & 65.3 & 56.7 & 63.1 & $-$8.6 & 6.4 \\\\\n264\t25K & 71.3 & 63.8 & 68.1 & $-$7.5 & 4.3 \\\\\n265\t50K & 74.5 & 68.1 & 72.3 & $-$6.4 & 4.2 \\\\\n266\t\\bottomrule\n267\t\\end{tabular}\n268\t\\caption{Scale ablation (single-seed, seed=42). TaxEI deficit persists at PALADIN-comparable scale.}\n269\t\\label{tab:scale}\n270\t\\end{table}\n271\t\n272\tScale improves all methods but does not eliminate TaxEI's deficit. At 50K---comparable to PALADIN's training scale---TaxEI still underperforms Vanilla by 6.4pp, and the variance gradient persists (TaxEI SD 5.5pp vs.\\ estimated Vanilla ${\\sim}$2.5pp). The taxonomy penalty decreases from 6.4pp to 4.2pp, suggesting that additional clean samples partially dilute the taxonomy interference, but cannot eliminate it. This rules out the hypothesis that insufficient scale explains our negative result.\n273\t\n274\t\\subsection{Few-Shot Recovery Prompting}\n275\t\\label{sec:fewshot}\n276\t\n277\tTo isolate whether the issue is the training signal itself (SFT gradient contamination) versus insufficient recovery knowledge, we compare SFT-based injection against inference-time few-shot recovery prompting (Table~\\ref{tab:fewshot}).\n278\t\n279\t\\begin{table}[t]\n280\t\\centering\n281\t\\small\n282\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n283\t\\toprule\n284\t\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{Success Rate} & \\textbf{vs.\\ Vanilla} \\\\\n285\t\\midrule\n286\tVanilla (no few-shot)     & 61.7\\% & --- \\\\\n287\tVanilla + 2-shot recovery & 63.8\\% & +2.1pp \\\\\n288\tVanilla + 3-shot recovery & 66.0\\% & +4.3pp \\\\\n289\tRandom SFT                & 63.1\\% & +1.4pp \\\\\n290\tTaxEI SFT                 & 56.7\\% & $-$5.0pp \\\\\n291\t\\bottomrule\n292\t\\end{tabular}\n293\t\\caption{Recovery method comparison (seed=42, 47 tasks).}\n294\t\\label{tab:fewshot}\n295\t\\end{table}\n296\t\n297\tVanilla + 3-shot recovery (66.0\\%) outperforms TaxEI SFT (56.7\\%) by 9.3pp, demonstrating that recovery information is valuable (+4.3pp over no-few-shot baseline) but SFT is the wrong delivery mechanism. Per-operator analysis reveals that few-shot gains are concentrated on C4 observation masking (+8.5pp) and P1 step-order swap (+4.2pp), while P2 sub-goal deletion shows zero gain---consistent with the hypothesis that globally cascading errors cannot be addressed through example-based recovery.\n298\t\n299\t\\subsection{Per-Operator Recovery Analysis}\n300\t\\label{sec:peroperator}\n301\t\n302\tWe evaluate recovery performance per error operator across three seeds (Table~\\ref{tab:peroperator}).\n303\t\n304\t\\begin{table}[t]\n305\t\\centering\n306\t\\small\n307\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n308\t\\toprule\n309\t\\textbf{Operator} & \\textbf{Vanilla} & \\textbf{TaxEI} & \\textbf{Random} & \\textbf{TaxEI$-$V} \\\\\n310\t\\midrule\n311\tC4 obs.\\ mask  & 60.6\\scriptsize{$\\pm$3.2} & 66.7\\scriptsize{$\\pm$3.5} & 63.6\\scriptsize{$\\pm$4.1} & +6.1pp \\\\\n312\tP1 step swap   & 58.3\\scriptsize{$\\pm$4.5} & 55.6\\scriptsize{$\\pm$5.2} & 58.3\\scriptsize{$\\pm$4.8} & $-$2.7pp \\\\\n313\tC1 ctx.\\ trunc.& 66.7\\scriptsize{$\\pm$3.8} & 61.1\\scriptsize{$\\pm$4.8} & 63.9\\scriptsize{$\\pm$4.2} & $-$5.6pp \\\\\n314\tP2 goal del.   & 55.6\\scriptsize{$\\pm$3.5} & 47.2\\scriptsize{$\\pm$4.0} & 52.8\\scriptsize{$\\pm$3.9} & $-$8.4pp \\\\\n315\t\\bottomrule\n316\t\\end{tabular}\n317\t\\caption{Per-operator recovery rate (\\%, 3-seed mean $\\pm$ SD).}\n318\t\\label{tab:peroperator}\n319\t\\end{table}\n320\t\n321\tThe operators form a clear gradient along error locality: C4 (local, re-observe) $\\to$ P1 (semi-local, re-order) $\\to$ C1 (diffuse, re-contextualize) $\\to$ P2 (global, re-plan). TaxEI advantage tracks this gradient monotonically: +6.1pp $\\to$ $-$2.7pp $\\to$ $-$5.6pp $\\to$ $-$8.4pp. C4 observation masking---the most ``tool-use-like'' error type, requiring a local, discrete recovery action---is the \\emph{only} operator where TaxEI consistently helps. P2 sub-goal deletion, which requires global re-planning, shows the largest deficit. This within-environment locality gradient mirrors the cross-domain contrast between tool-use (PALADIN, local recovery) and planning (TaxEI, global recovery).\n322\t\n323\t\\subsection{Statistical Analysis}\n324\t\\label{sec:stats}\n325\t\n326\tTable~\\ref{tab:stats} summarizes the pooled statistical tests incorporating all multi-seed dose-response data.\n327\t\n328\t\\begin{table}[t]\n329\t\\centering\n330\t\\small\n331\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n332\t\\toprule\n333\t\\textbf{Test} & \\textbf{Original} & \\textbf{Updated} \\\\\n334\t\\midrule\n335\tPerm.\\ TaxEI vs.\\ Van. & $p{=}0.054$ & $p{=}0.031$* \\\\\n336\tPerm.\\ TaxEI vs.\\ Rand. & $p{=}0.170$ & $p{=}0.042$* \\\\\n337\tPaired $t$ TaxEI vs.\\ Rand. & $p{=}0.034$ & $p{=}0.024$* \\\\\n338\tPaired $t$ TaxEI vs.\\ Van.  & $p{=}0.32$  & $p{=}0.18$~NS \\\\\n339\tVariance ratio $F$-test & --- & $p{=}0.038$* \\\\\n340\tBootstrap 95\\% CI overlap & Yes & No \\\\\n341\tCohen's $d$ (TaxEI vs.\\ Van.) & 0.38 & 0.42 \\\\\n342\t\\bottomrule\n343\t\\end{tabular}\n344\t\\caption{Statistical tests. *: $p < 0.05$. Effective $N$ increased from 141 to 423 task-level observations with multi-seed dose-response data.}\n345\t\\label{tab:stats}\n346\t\\end{table}\n347\t\n348\tThe pooled permutation test for TaxEI vs.\\ Vanilla reaches $p{=}0.031$ (effective $N{=}423$), crossing the $\\alpha{=}0.05$ threshold. Bootstrap 95\\% CIs are non-overlapping: TaxEI [52.8\\%, 60.6\\%] vs.\\ Vanilla [62.0\\%, 68.6\\%]. The variance ratio $F$-test ($p{=}0.038$) formally confirms that TaxEI exhibits significantly higher cross-seed variance than Vanilla (ratio 6.79$\\times$). Cohen's $d{=}0.42$ indicates a medium effect size. Notably, the paired $t$-test for TaxEI vs.\\ Vanilla remains non-significant ($p{=}0.18$), because seed-level analysis ($n{=}9$) lacks power against the large inter-seed variance---this asymmetry itself supports our core claim that the primary effect is \\emph{increased instability} rather than systematic mean degradation.\n349\t\n350\t\\section{Discussion}\n351\t\n352\t\\paragraph{Why does error injection fail in planning?}\n353\t\n354\tOur results converge on a task-structural explanation organized along four dimensions (Table~\\ref{tab:structural}).\n355\t\n356\t\\begin{table}[t]\n357\t\\centering\n358\t\\small\n359\t\\begin{tabular}{p{2.2cm}p{2.3cm}p{2.5cm}}\n360\t\\toprule\n361\t\\textbf{Dimension} & \\textbf{Tool-Use}~\\checkmark & \\textbf{Planning}~$\\times$ \\\\\n362\t\\midrule\n363\tError observ. & Explicit (API codes) & Implicit (valid-looking) \\\\\n364\tRecovery space & Bounded (retry/switch) & Unbounded (re-plan) \\\\\n365\tError locality & Single turn & Cascading \\\\\n366\tFeedback & Per-tool signal & Sparse reward \\\\\n367\t\\bottomrule\n368\t\\end{tabular}\n369\t\\caption{Task-structural comparison between tool-use and embodied planning.}\n370\t\\label{tab:structural}\n371\t\\end{table}\n372\t\n373\tThe per-operator analysis (\\S\\ref{sec:peroperator}) provides a micro-level version of this macro contrast: within the same environment, error locality determines whether injection helps (+6.1pp for local C4) or hurts ($-$8.4pp for global P2). This suggests that the boundary is not between ``domains'' but between error \\emph{types}---specifically, whether recovery requires local action substitution or global plan revision.\n374\t\n375\t\\paragraph{The taxonomy penalty as systematic bias.}\n376\t\n377\tThe constant ${\\sim}$6.4pp gap between TaxEI and Random across 9 independent observations (3 seeds $\\times$ 3 doses, SD $\\approx$ 0.4pp) is not explainable by noise. Training dynamics show that taxonomy-labeled samples produce coherent but wrong gradient directions (cosine 0.28), while random labels produce diffuse interference (cosine 0.35). The qualitative finding that 25\\% of TaxEI failures are ``operator replays''---the model reproducing taxonomy-specific error behaviors during clean evaluation---provides the behavioral manifestation of this gradient-level phenomenon.\n378\t\n379\t\\paragraph{Implications for the community.}\n380\t\n381\tOur results suggest three actionable guidelines: (1) Error injection methods should be validated with $\\geq$3 seeds, as single-seed results can mask a 2.6$\\times$ variance increase. (2) In planning domains, inference-time recovery prompting (+4.3pp) is preferable to SFT-based injection ($-$5.0pp). (3) If error injection is attempted in planning, restricting to locally recoverable error types (observation masking) may yield benefits while avoiding the harmful effects of globally cascading errors.\n382\t\n383\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n384\t\n385\tOur comparison with PALADIN involves confounds beyond task structure: base model, training scale, and the presence of inference-time retrieval. While the scale ablation (\\S\\ref{sec:scale}) and few-shot experiment (\\S\\ref{sec:fewshot}) partially address scale and retrieval confounds respectively, a fully controlled cross-domain study remains future work. ALFWorld's 47 test tasks limit per-operator statistical power. The WebShop replication uses only 2 seeds.\n386\t\n387\t\\section{Conclusion}\n388\t\n389\tTaxonomy-guided error injection, successful in tool-use domains, degrades embodied planning agent training. The mechanism is twofold: a universal threshold-plateau effect where error injection beyond ${\\sim}$15\\% collapses performance regardless of injection method, and a constant ${\\sim}$6.4pp taxonomy penalty where structured error labels teach specific wrong behaviors. The boundary between success and failure aligns with error locality: only locally recoverable errors benefit from injection training. These findings establish task-structural boundary conditions for failure-augmented training and caution against assuming that error injection transfers across agent domains.\n390\t\n391\t\\begin{thebibliography}{9}\n392\t\n393\t\\bibitem{chen2025paladin}\n394\tChen et al.\n395\t\\newblock PALADIN: Leveraging Tool-Use Failure Taxonomies for Robust LLM Agent Training.\n396\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2509.25238}, 2025.\n397\t\n398\t\\bibitem{zhang2025eef}\n399\tZhang et al."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01SfVBGkmTnqCYQUegZczMa7","session_id":"3dbddd0c-fe11-423e-bc66-5161ab0e513e","uuid":"440cb021-8431-41f4-8327-aaa9ce764333","timestamp":"2026-05-16T16:17:01.613Z"}
